description:
FILED s537a (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-11) as #4325's residue.  s537a fixed the `eval { }` half (an eval that is the whole void statement takes p-eval-block-discarded: no leave copy).  The sub-call half is the same mechanism and is NOT fixed:

    my $n = 0;
    sub TIESCALAR { bless \(my $thing = pop), shift }
    sub FETCH { $n++; ${$_[0]} }
    tie $tied, main => $untied = [];
    sub f { bless $tied, 'Q' }
    $n = 0; f(); print "fetches: $n\n";        # perl 1   PCL 2
    sub g { $tied }
    $n = 0; g(); print "fetches: $n\n";        # perl 0   PCL 1

WHY IT IS NOT A ONE-LINER: the copy is p-sub-frame's %p-leavesub, run INSIDE the callee, which reads only *wantarray*.  %p-leavesub's docstring records the measurement: PCL's void regime binds :void around a WHOLE discarded statement, so an inner call whose value IS consumed (`my $r = \f();`, `mod(f())` -- emitted (p-void-ctx (pl-mod (pl-f))), the argument call unwrapped) sees :void too; skipping the copy on :void broke two probe rows.  The eval fix could key on the SHAPE at macroexpansion ((p-void-ctx (p-eval-block ...)) -- the eval is the sole form); a sub call cannot, because its arguments are evaluated in the same dynamic extent as the call.  A design needs a "value discarded" signal that the callee reads and its arguments do not see -- e.g. the void statement's call emitted with its argument forms evaluated first and the call made under a distinct binding -- and every reader of *wantarray* left unchanged.  Measure call-heavy bench rows (gcdrec, symref, ovlsub, fib: %p-leavesub's docstring) before and after.  Probe: scratch/s537a/p/m5c.pl row 3.
